WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:00.930 --> 00:06.370
The behaviors we perform routinely, whether consciously or unconsciously, are our habits.

2
00:07.010 --> 00:12.230
Each of us has been developing habits for as long as we've been on this planet, however long that may be.

3
00:12.870 --> 00:16.160
From how we brush our teeth to how we manage our email traffic.

4
00:16.550 --> 00:21.280
We've formed habits in all aspects of our life by doing the same behaviors over and over again.

5
00:22.150 --> 00:25.600
This includes the specific habits we have when it comes to learning.

6
00:26.470 --> 00:31.040
Take a moment right now to reflect on the learning habits you've developed over the course of your life.

7
00:32.520 --> 00:37.380
For example, I remember when I was studying ancient civilizations for the first time.

8
00:38.090 --> 00:42.270
My older sister Anna happened to be taking a college seminar on Greek literature.

9
00:42.790 --> 00:48.160
And all I wanted to do was ask questions, read, and learn more about what she was studying.

10
00:48.650 --> 00:50.310
I even started practicing Greek.

11
00:51.080 --> 00:55.910
As a kid, this meant that I always found the time to explore the things I found exciting.

12
00:56.680 --> 01:03.200
As an adult, we'd call this particular learning habit planning and prioritizing practice, but the idea is the same.

13
01:03.650 --> 01:06.690
It's important to create the time and space for regular learning.

14
01:08.200 --> 01:11.750
I have far less pleasant memories from my chemistry class in college.

15
01:12.360 --> 01:18.020
There were just so many other things I'd rather do than spend hours analyzing the results of my last experiment.

16
01:18.760 --> 01:25.890
Needless to say, I always found myself writing my lab reports at the last minute, and probably didn't learn as much as I could have.

17
01:27.010 --> 01:32.740
All these years later, I may have gotten a decent grade in the end, but I don't remember anything.

18
01:33.350 --> 01:36.800
And the infamous procrastination has stuck with me in the workplace.

19
01:37.800 --> 01:40.770
I've developed even more learning habits at work over the years.

20
01:41.250 --> 01:45.600
Some are good, and others… well, there's room for improvement.

21
01:46.210 --> 01:52.070
For example, a positive learning habit is that I always take the time to share my knowledge and teach others.

22
01:52.710 --> 01:54.660
Ask my colleagues, Kofi and Maria.

23
01:55.210 --> 01:59.970
We've spent so many hours together this past year working on an upgrade of our HR systems.

24
02:00.900 --> 02:07.910
Despite the fact that I proactively share my knowledge with others, funny enough, I'm terrible at asking for help myself.

25
02:08.490 --> 02:11.360
So I'm working to change that, and instead

26
02:11.720 --> 02:17.760
develop the better learning habit of reflecting on where I need support, and then actually asking for it.

27
02:19.460 --> 02:23.230
When it comes to the habits we've cultivated over time, some are purposeful,

28
02:23.590 --> 02:26.110
like practicing 15 minutes of yoga a day,

29
02:26.530 --> 02:30.690
and some are subconscious, like driving the same way to work every morning.

30
02:31.400 --> 02:36.480
And when it comes to learning, research clearly points to the behaviors that are the most beneficial.

31
02:37.320 --> 02:44.350
If you consciously turn those positive learning behaviors into habits, you will be able to learn more efficiently and effectively.

32
02:44.970 --> 02:47.750
Why? Because a fresh start can be exciting.

33
02:48.330 --> 02:52.350
But with time, the novelty fades away and motivation can decrease.

34
02:53.190 --> 03:00.070
Building good habits automates beneficial behaviors, decreasing the amount of energy you need to keep yourself motivated.

35
03:01.060 --> 03:05.090
This energy you have conserved can then be applied to grow in other areas.

36
03:05.510 --> 03:07.750
We call this cognitive efficiency:

37
03:08.490 --> 03:12.930
your ability to reach learning or other goals through optimal use of mental resources.

38
03:13.480 --> 03:16.070
In other words, you've saved precious brain power.

39
03:17.350 --> 03:23.430
In the face of today's constant change, our success is fueled by how quickly we respond to challenges,

40
03:24.070 --> 03:26.630
acquire new skills, and adapt.

41
03:27.490 --> 03:30.590
And good learning habits support our ability to do these things.

42
03:31.560 --> 03:35.940
With each good learning habit we develop, and with each bad one we leave behind,

43
03:36.290 --> 03:40.700
we're better able to navigate our world, both now and in the future.
